    "evidence_text": "We present a new context representation for convolutional neural networks for relation classification (extended middle context).\n\nFinally, we show that combining convolutional and recurrent neural networks using a simple voting scheme is accurate enough to improve results.\n\nWe make the following contributions: (1) We propose extended middle context, a new context representation for CNNs for relation classification.\n\nInput: Extended Middle Context\tOne of our contributions is a new input representation especially designed for relation classification.\n\nHence, we propose to use two contexts: (1) a combination of the left context, the left entity and the middle context; and (2) a combination of the middle context, the right entity and the right context.\n\nBased on the representation of the current input word and the previous history vector, a new history is computed.\n\nWord Representations\tWords are represented by concatenated vectors: a word embedding and a position feature vector.\n\nAs input it uses the middle context.\n\nIn the next step, we investigate how this result changes if we successively add further features to our CNN: multi-windows for convolution (window sizes:\n\nsentence, the CNN extracts the most informative n-grams for the relation and only considers their resulting activations.\n\nFor convolutional neural networks, we presented a new context representation for relation classification.",
